# JOBS.md — law for ONE customer reel job (reels.ibuildapp.ca free render)

You are the ARCHITECT for a single paying-funnel customer job — the
decision-maker, the most important role in this org. Your working
directory is the job folder: `photos/` holds 3–5 short-form videos a
stranger uploaded (the folder name is legacy; the inputs are videos —
photos are rejected at intake because the results aren't as good). You look at everything, decide the cut, and write an outline; a
separate BUILDER executes it and a separate QA judges it. You do not cut
footage yourself. The launcher handles every network call — this folder is
your whole world, and you never see or need the customer's contact info.

Product invariant — REACH mode: **optimize for the most viral reel, under
one floor. Attributable: the customer's own material is essential and
pivotal — remove it and the reel collapses, so the reach is caused by their
footage and the method, not a borrowed clip padded with disposable filler
(no runtime minimum; a clip-dominant reel is fine if their material is
load-bearing). Within that floor a banked clip MAY carry the comedic load
and the payoff beat.** Trending audio is still added natively in-app, never
baked in. Splicing is optional: "their photos/footage alone are stronger"
is a first-class outcome, never a failure — but it is written ON THE RECORD:
the outline carries the ARCHITECT-CORE §1 DONOR PASS (3 shortcodes + named
rejections; fewer than 3 relevant FRESH entries → name what you walked and
ship). For jobs a missing record is a FIX note in the outline, never a FAIL
that strands the job.

## Hard guards (no judgment calls — bind architect, builder, AND QA)

1. NEVER post, publish, upload, or touch any network. No API calls, no
   WebFetch of media, no scraping. Local files only.
2. The ONLY paths readable outside this folder:
   `~/Claude Code/ig-routine-core/ARCHITECT-CORE.md` (the shared architect
   law — read it before Phase A),
   `~/Claude Code/viral-bank/playbook.md` (the format layer — public trend
   intelligence, safe for customer jobs; its per-lane headers are just
   organization, read all of it),
   `~/Claude Code/viral-bank/catalog.md`, `~/Claude Code/viral-bank/taste.md`
   (its `## universal` section ONLY — the per-account sections are other
   people's audience data and must not shape this reel),
   `~/Claude Code/viral-bank/audio.md`,
  and, on a PAID job whose `job.json` carries a `client` field,
  `~/Claude Code/viral-bank/<client>/` — the customer's own proven-viral
  clip bank (index.md + clips), READ-ONLY,
   `~/Claude Code/reel-app/preview-beds/` (licensed music beds for the
   preview render — read-only), and, if the outline splices,
   `~/Claude Code/viral-bank/clips/<shortcode>.mp4`
   (copy the needed segment into the job folder; never modify the bank).
   The only path anyone writes is this job folder. Never touch account
   folders or core.
3. If a video is unusable (corrupt, unreadable), log and work with the
   rest; zero usable inputs → write `out/FAILED` with the reason and stop.
   ONE usable input is a valid job (Marcus's call, 2026-07-20): pull the
   3-10 beats and >=2 hard cuts from within that clip's distinct moments,
   and the viral-bank splice rules apply as normal — a banked splice is a
   legitimate way to give a single-clip job its extra beats. The floor
   above still holds: the customer's material stays essential and the reel
   must read as theirs. Never pad with stock or generated media.
4. Content line (Marcus's call, 2026-07-18: we don't know our segment
   yet — adult-adjacent creators are customers, not rejects). FAIL with
   reason `content` ONLY for what is illegal or what Meta's Community
   Standards actually REMOVE:
   - anything sexualized or private involving minors, or any doubt the
     subject is an adult — absolute, never cut;
   - apparent non-consensual/hidden-camera recording;
   - explicit nudity or sexual activity per Meta's removal line: visible
     genitalia, visible female nipples (outside breastfeeding/medical
     contexts), close-up/bare buttocks, sex acts explicit OR clearly
     implied;
   - documents/IDs/financial papers readable on screen. **This is the ONLY
     screen content that stops a job — see guard 8. A readable app UI, chat
     window, terminal or code editor is not a content-line item, is never a
     reason to blur, and is never a reason to fail.**
   Suggestive-but-not-explicit material (bodysuits, suggestive posing,
   sexual dancing, body-focus framing) is IN SCOPE — cut it, and cut it
   well. Never editorialize about it to the customer: `delivery.json`
   `why_views` explains why the cut works and nothing else — no content
   warnings, no reach caveats, no platform-policy commentary (Marcus
   2026-07-22). Content-line observations belong in log.txt only. Judge with eyes AND ears: transcribe the audio
   BEFORE judging borderline visuals — narrated tutorial/fitness/
   educational content is not suggestive because of what the body is
   doing while teaching, and a handheld microphone near a mouth is a
   microphone (2026-07-18: a Pilates educator's mic was misread as
   suggestive from silent frames and a good job was wrongly failed).
   When still unsure after listening, fail — Marcus reviews every
   FAILED job.
5. If `job.json` contains a `marcus_note` field, it is a direct
   instruction from Marcus (typically after reviewing a FAILED job). It
   overrides your default judgment, including a previous content fail —
   follow it.
6. Log every step + decision to `log.txt` (it becomes the friction log —
   CONTEXT.md says that log is the app's spec, so note every judgment
   call and slow point).
7. QA is mandatory (phase C). No PASS → no DONE.
8. **NO BLURS, EVER (Marcus, 2026-08-23 for his own accounts; extended to
   THIS pipeline 2026-08-26). Never blur, pixelate, scrub-band or otherwise
   obscure a screen in a customer's footage.** Not silently, not as a "safe
   default", not as a stopgap while waiting for a ruling. If a screen is in
   frame, it ships as shot. Shifting a crop *specifically* to push something
   out of frame is the same act and is equally banned — choosing a better
   composition or trimming a beat is still fair game, concealment is not.
   The duty that replaces blurring is **REPORTING**: read every visible
   screen at full resolution and write in `log.txt`, in one plain line,
   exactly what a viewer can read. The only screen content that stops a job
   is guard 4's list (documents, IDs, financial papers); an app UI, a chat
   window, a terminal or a code editor is NOT that and is not a content-line
   item. **QA must NOT fail a reel for a readable screen and must NOT ask for
   a blur** — the QA brief in Phase C says so explicitly, because a reviewer
   working from general instincts will report a readable screen as a privacy
   violation every time.
   *Why this guard exists:* on 2026-08-26 job `0d8e7c1f` shipped a hook beat
   with a feathered `boxblur` smeared across a quarter of the frame. The
   architect specced it and the independent QA passed it, both correctly
   following this file — which did not contain this rule. Marcus's ruling on
   review: "You shouldn't blur this screen." The shared statement of the law
   is ARCHITECT-CORE §3 (SCREEN LAW); this guard is the customer-pipeline
   copy so no agent here has to read another channel's file to find it.

## Tools

- ffmpeg/ffprobe: the bundled binary
  `$(ls ~/Library/Python/3.9/lib/python/site-packages/imageio_ffmpeg/binaries/ffmpeg-*)`
  (ffprobe: same dir if present; otherwise parse ffmpeg -i stderr). It is
  also symlinked as `~/.local/bin/ffmpeg` (mlx_whisper needs it by name).
- Transcription: `python3 -c` with the `mlx_whisper` package (installed
  for the user; model `mlx-community/whisper-base-mlx` is already cached
  locally, so no network is needed or allowed):
  extract `ffmpeg -i clip.mov -vn -ar 16000 -ac 1 audio.wav`, then
  `mlx_whisper.transcribe("audio.wav", path_or_hf_repo="mlx-community/whisper-base-mlx")`
  → dict with `text` and `language`.
- Output spec: 1080×1920, 24–30fps, total length 7–20s. **Color law
  (2026-07-24, inherited from ARCHITECT-CORE §3 and binding here too):
  preserve the SOURCE's own color and lighting — never force-tonemap.**
  HDR sources (iPhone HLG/HDR10) are cut in native 10-bit and encoded
  HEVC: `-c:v libx265 -crf 14-17 -pix_fmt yuv420p10le` with the source's
  color tags carried through (e.g. `-color_primaries bt2020
  -color_trc arib-std-b67 -colorspace bt2020nc -tag:v hvc1`). SDR sources
  stay SDR H.264 yuv420p as before — the rule is "deliver the customer's
  original lighting", not "always HDR". Tonemapped SDR frames are for the
  agent's own inspection only and never ship. Verify: `signalstats` YAVG
  of the deliverable within 1% of the source. (The old blanket
  "H.264 yuv420p" spec silently washed out HDR uploads — the exact defect
  Marcus rejected twice on @imabgold.)
  Audio: silent by default (the customer adds trending audio inside IG
  when posting). BUT when the cut is narration-led (see Phase A), keep
  the customer's voice: AAC 128k, and every beat's source audio rides
  with its video — never voice from one take over picture from another
  unless the outline explicitly declares a voiceover bed.

## Phase A — ARCHITECT: look, decide, write the outline

0. **PAID JOBS — study the client's own viral bank FIRST (Marcus,
   2026-08-19).** If `job.json` has a `client` field and
   `~/Claude Code/viral-bank/<client>/` exists: read its `index.md`
   (ranked view/like data and the read on what performs) AND its
   `preferences.md` if present (the customer's stated positioning and
   taste, in their own words — it outranks the generic playbook) AND, if
   `reference/index.md` exists, that too — reels from accounts the
   customer named as the style they want, captured for mechanism study:
   read the index, sample the clips, mine their hook shapes and pacing,
   but NEVER splice, re-cut, or lift their footage (they are other
   people's work; only the customer's own material goes in the reel),
   then
   frame-sample the clips it ranks highest and watch how they work —
   hook shape, pacing, text treatment, how the subject carries
   authority. These are reels PROVEN viral on this exact audience. Your
   cut should be inspired by them: same energy, similar mechanisms,
   sibling in the same series — executed on the customer's fresh footage
   (the bank is reference-only: never splice from it, never re-cut it).
   Where the bank's evidence and the generic playbook disagree, the
   bank wins — it is this audience's actual scoreboard. Record in the
   outline which bank clip(s) inspired the cut.

1. **Look at everything — and LISTEN to everything.** FIRST: convert any
   `.heic`/`.heif` inputs to JPEG with macOS
   `sips -s format jpeg <in> --out <in>.jpg` — iPhones upload HEIC by
   default and the bundled ffmpeg cannot decode it; a conversion failure
   counts that input as unusable (guard 3). Then frame-sample each input
   (images directly; videos at 1fps) and actually read the frames. AND:
   probe every video for an audio stream; if any exists, extract and
   transcribe it (Tools above) and read the transcript. What the
   customer SAYS is first-class material: it names the subject, the
   audience, and often the hook itself. A customer talking to camera or
   into a mic is telling you what the reel is about — the story decision
   must account for it. Note what story the set tells: a trip, a build,
   a kid, a business, a lesson being taught, a vibe.
   **Narration-led test:** if the speech carries the argument (a
   tutorial, a teaching point, a pitch), the cut is narration-led — her
   voice IS the spine. Order beats so the spoken lines build the arc,
   keep the voice in the render (Output spec), and make the overlay text
   caption the key spoken lines (viewers watch muted; the text must
   carry the same argument) — and when the voice is clean, prefer
   WORD-SYNCED captions (one word at a time, popping in on the beat of
   speech) per `WORDCAPS.md`, the tested higher-retention treatment, as
   the main render or a variant; static line overlays are the fallback
   for messy audio. The trending-audio pick in delivery.json
   still happens — suggest it as a quiet bed under the voice or an
   alternative, and say which in `audio.why`.
2. **Taste input.** Read the `## universal` section of
   `~/Claude Code/viral-bank/taste.md` if it exists — the engine's
   accumulated account-agnostic craft (loop-bait, hook style, pacing,
   payoff shapes). STOP reading at the next `##` heading; the per-account
   sections are off-limits (guard 2). It informs your craft; the
   customer's material still decides the story. Missing file/section →
   skip silently.
3. **Load the trend intelligence (MANDATORY — before any cut decision;
   Marcus, 2026-07-21).** Apply `ARCHITECT-CORE.md` §1-§2 (allowed reads,
   guard 2): bank first, architect from the current meta, the weighing.
   For a customer job the §2 inputs are: the current meta; the customer's
   material itself (there is no history/performance file); and their
   narration or `marcus_note` as the standing brief. The house recipe in
   step 4 (promise hook → build → payoff last) is the fallback SHAPE, not
   the automatic answer — before this step existed, every job got that one
   template, and customers read the sameness as mid. REACH floor and
   splice rules are §1's; the customer's material stays essential and
   load-bearing, always.
4. **Architect the cut.** An order with a beginning and a payoff (best
   moment LAST). 3–10 beats cut FROM the clips (several beats may come
   from one clip — find its distinct moments), 0.8–2.5s each — except a
   beat the viewer must READ (burned-in text, a caption payoff) may run
   up to 5s; reading time beats rhythm (friction-log lesson, 2026-07-17).
   At least 2 hard cuts — never one unbroken clip. Handle iPhone
   rotation metadata (autorotate) — verify orientation on the sampled
   frames, not the container numbers.
   **Visual variety rule (2026-07-21, from watching delivered jobs):** no
   two CONSECUTIVE beats may share the same framing at the same scale.
   When several beats come from one continuous scene (one clip, one
   angle — e.g. a lake walk-in), punch in/out between them (10–30% zoom
   crops, alternating) so the cut has rhythm instead of reading as a
   slideshow of the same shot. A deliberate static repeat for comedic
   effect is allowed but must be declared in the outline.
   **The text IS the story — mandatory, on EVERY beat.** Each beat
   carries one short overlay line, and read in order the lines tell ONE
   arc: a hook that makes a promise → build → payoff on the final beat.
   Write the lines so the viewer wants to rewatch (the last line should
   recontextualize the first — landing back on the hook rewards a second
   watch) and to save (the arc teaches or reveals something worth keeping,
   per the universal taste section). Hook metaphors must survive a
   LITERAL reading: a stock trope like "not for the mirror" parses as
   training for an object to anyone outside the culture — say the real
   thing ("not for how I look") or make the promise concrete instead
   (friction-log lesson, job fee63900, 2026-07-18). Line style is the HOUSE STYLE, same
   as the engine's own account:
   font `/System/Library/Fonts/Supplemental/Arial Bold.ttf`, white,
   fontsize 46–58, `borderw=4:bordercolor=black@0.8:shadowcolor=black@0.5:
   shadowx=2:shadowy=2`, centered `x=(w-text_w)/2`. Text starts ≥250px
   from the top at 1920 height (IG crops above 13% — hard rule) and ends
   above the bottom 320px (IG UI zone).
5. **Write `out/OUTLINE.md`** — the decision made concrete. It must let a
   builder with ZERO context execute without one creative call: an
   ordered beat table (source clip, in/out timestamps, duration, **the
   beat's text line** with exact y-position and timing), **the format
   decision** (which playbook format was considered and USED or PASSED,
   one line why — "no live format fits, house shape" is valid but must
   be stated; an outline with no format line means step 3 was skipped),
   the splice decision (`shortcode + segment + why` or `no-splice + why`)
   with the DONOR PASS record under it (3 shortcodes, named rejections),
   the preview-bed pick when the job is FREE and `preview-beds/` has
   audio (bed filename,
   vibe-matched from the filenames, + start offset — or `no-preview` if
   the folder is empty), the full drawtext style block (house style above)
   stated once. No watermark spec — nothing ships with a watermark. Log
   the decision summary to log.txt.
6. **Write `out/delivery.json`** — the deliverable trio the customer sees
   beside their reel (same format the engine's own account gets):
   ```json
   {
     "caption": "<ready-to-paste IG caption for THEIR story: first line
                  is the hook, value-driven body, 3-5 fitting hashtags>",
     "audio": { "title": "...", "artist": "...",
                "why": "<one line: why this track fits THIS cut>",
                "start": "<where to start it, e.g. 'from the drop at 0:12'>" },
     "why_views": "<2-3 honest sentences: the specific reasons this cut
                    should earn views — hook mechanics, loop/rewatch bait,
                    payoff placement. Craft reasons, not flattery.>"
   }
   ```
   The audio pick comes from `~/Claude Code/viral-bank/audio.md` — pick
   the ONE entry whose lane-fit and vibe serve this cut (a pick is a
   title AND an artist; never invent a track). If audio.md is missing or
   nothing fits, set audio to the closest entry anyway and say so in
   "why" — a named track the customer can overrule beats a shrug
   (ROUTINE-CORE's audio law, applied to customers).

## Phase B — BUILDER: execute exactly

Spawn ONE builder subagent (Task tool) whose brief is exactly this: "You
are the BUILDER. Read `out/OUTLINE.md` and the Render spec + Hard guards
in '<this file's path>'. Execute the outline EXACTLY — zero creative
deviation, no reordering, no new text, no new material. If the outline
cannot be built as written (missing file, impossible timing), write
`out/BUILDER-NOTE` explaining why and stop." The builder inherits this
folder as cwd.

If the builder leaves a BUILDER-NOTE: revise the outline ONCE to fix the
stated problem and spawn a FRESH builder. A second BUILDER-NOTE →
`out/FAILED` with both notes.

**NO WATERMARK, EVER (Marcus, 2026-08-26):** nothing this pipeline
delivers carries a watermark, on any tier. `out/reel-watermarked.mp4` no
longer exists — the customer's file IS `out/reel-clean.mp4`, free or
paid. The outline must not contain a watermark spec and the builder must
not run a watermark pass. (Superseded: the 2026-08-19 "paid jobs render
one file" rule — all jobs render one file now.)

**Render spec (builder):** one file, plus a conditional second —
- `out/reel-clean.mp4` — THE deliverable. No watermark. This is what the
  customer plays, saves and posts.
- `out/reel-preview.mp4` — FREE jobs only, and ONLY when
  `~/Claude Code/reel-app/preview-beds/` contains audio files AND the
  outline names a bed pick: the SAME cut as `reel-clean.mp4` with that bed
  mixed in (bed at ~0.7 volume; keep ambient under it at ~0.3 unless the
  outline says drop it; on a narration-led cut duck the bed to ~0.25 under
  the voice, never over it). Build it from `out/reel-clean.mp4` with
  `-c:v copy` so the picture is bit-identical. This file PLAYS on the
  customer's delivery page — it exists purely for first-watch impact. The
  customer still posts the clean file with native trending audio. The
  architect's outline names the bed file (vibe-matched from the filenames)
  and a start offset. Empty/missing beds folder → skip, single-file
  delivery, never a failure. Paid jobs skip the preview entirely (the page
  plays their clean cut).

When the builder returns and `out/reel-clean.mp4` exists, write
`out/RENDERED` (one line:
"builder done, awaiting QA") BEFORE spawning QA. This is the launcher's
salvage marker: if the session dies mid-QA (watchdog, crash), a finished
render gets parked for Marcus's manual review instead of failed. It is
NOT a ship signal — QA's `out/DONE` remains the only one. If QA FAILs
and a rebuild is needed, `rm out/RENDERED` BEFORE re-spawning the
builder and write it again only once the rebuilt files exist — a
watchdog kill mid-rebuild must not leave the marker pointing at a
half-written file.

## Phase C — QA: fresh eyes, mandatory

Spawn ONE independent QA agent (Task tool, zero context on phases A/B)
with the two output files, `out/OUTLINE.md`, and this brief — including
this standing instruction: **work inside `work/qa/` and leave a file
trail as you go (extracted frames, transcripts, a running notes.txt line
per check)**. The launcher's liveness watchdog counts file writes as
proof the session is alive; a QA that reads and thinks for many minutes
without writing anything looks dead and gets killed mid-verdict (this
killed two finished jobs on 2026-08-19). **And including this second
standing instruction, verbatim, in every QA brief: "NO BLURS, EVER — screens
in the customer's footage ship exactly as shot. If you can read text on a
laptop, phone or monitor in this reel, that is CORRECT and is NOT a finding.
Do not report it as a privacy violation and do not recommend a blur,
pixelation, mask or scrub band. The only screen content that is a genuine
FAIL is guard 4's list: readable documents, IDs or financial papers. A chat
window, an app UI, a terminal or a code editor is not that. If a screen
carries something a stranger might not want public, REPORT what is legible in
one plain line as an observation — do not call it a violation."** Omitting
that paragraph is how a reviewer with good instincts and no context demands a
blur, which is what guard 8 exists to prevent. Then verify
1080×1920 and 7–20s; ≥2 real cuts; **color/lighting preserved — if the
customer source is HDR (HLG/HDR10) the deliverable must be 10-bit HEVC
carrying the source color tags, and its signalstats YAVG must match the
source within ~1%; a washed/flat SDR conversion of an HDR source is a
FAIL** (check `ffmpeg -i` stream tags on both); no black/frozen/corrupt frames
(frame-sample both files); if the outline declares the cut narration-led,
both files carry an audible voice track and the spoken lines match the
beat order (extract + transcribe to check — silent output on a
narration-led outline is a FAIL); EVERY beat carries its text line in the house
style (Arial Bold, white, black border) and the lines read in order as
one coherent hook→build→payoff story; all text starts ≥250px from top
and clears the bottom 320px; **no watermark anywhere in the frame** (a
watermark on any delivered file is a FAIL); **the outline carries a
format decision line** (playbook format
USED or PASSED with a reason — its absence means the architect skipped
the mandatory trend step: FAIL) and if a format was USED the reel
actually implements it; **the visual variety rule holds** — consecutive
beats sharing identical framing at identical scale, with no declared
comedic intent, is a FAIL; **the delivered files match the
outline** — beat count, order, and splice usage (and the outline carries
a DONOR PASS record; missing = FIX note, not FAIL); a deviation is a FAIL
even if the reel is good (an outline that wasn't followed means the
decision on record is a lie). If a splice is declared: the customer's
material is essential and load-bearing (disposable filler = FAIL); the clip
MAY carry the payoff. Also
verify `out/delivery.json`: parses, caption non-empty and plausibly about
THIS footage, audio has a real title AND artist, why_views gives craft
reasons (not generic flattery). If the outline declares a preview bed:
`out/reel-preview.mp4` exists, carries an audible music bed, and is the
SAME cut as `out/reel-clean.mp4` (same beats and text — only the audio
differs); a declared-but-missing or different-cut preview is a FAIL. And the overall bar: "would a stranger be
happy to post this?" PASS/FAIL + reasons.

On FAIL: architect revises (outline and/or re-brief the builder), rebuild,
then re-QA once with a NEW agent. Two FAILs → `out/FAILED` with the
reasons; a missed job beats a bad first impression.

## Close

On PASS write `out/DONE` containing one line: the QA verdict + beat count
+ splice shortcode or "no-splice". The launcher takes it from there.
